details:
(a) whether West Bengal, which was absent from tripartite MoUs signed by DoPT with 28 States under Mission Karmayogi, has signed the MoU and if so, the number of civil servants currently registered on iGoT Karmayogi platform there;
(b) the specific capacity building plan developed for the State's civil servants under Mission Karmayogi, number of courses identified for the officials across domain, behavioural and functional competency areas and targets set for course completions during 2026-27; and
(c) the support extended by Capacity Building Commission to the State Government for integrating iGoT with State HR systems and implementing role-based competency framework?

(a) whether the West Bengal Government has submitted its Annual Action Plan under Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) for Financial Year 2026-27 for which Centre allocated a record ?2,500 crore nationally and projects approved for West Bengal given eight lakh hectares of inland water bodies, 158 km coastline and Sundarbans fisheries;
(b) funds sanctioned and released to West Bengal under PMMSY since May, 2026 for aquaculture, seaweed cultivation and fishermen welfare; and
(c) steps to ensure West Bengal effectively utilises PMMSY funds given underutilisation previously?

(a) whether West Bengal's Budget 2026-27 identifies land for two new Kendriya Vidyalayas in Jhargram and Bankura and whether Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan (KVS) has received formal communication from State Government transferring such land as required under KVS's standard sanction conditions;
(b) if land transfer is complete, whether KVS proposes to issue sanction orders for these schools, and expected academic session for operationalisation; and
(c) whether Navodaya Vidyalaya Samiti (NVS) has received proposals from West Bengal for new Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas in South 24 Parganas, Malda, Jhargram and Purba Bardhaman identified in State Budget, and the sanction status, land transfer position and estimated timeline?

(a) whether it is a fact that as of August 2025, 170.4 acres of Syama Prasad Mookerjee Port land was under encroachment by 706 encroachers;
(b) the current updated status of encroachment following active reclamation drives undertaken with the cooperation of the West Bengal Government since May 2026;
(c) the total area of port land reclaimed, number of encroachers evicted and area still pending reclamation as of date; and
(d) the revenue potential of the reclaimed land and the timeline for its productive monetisation through warehousing, ship repair or riverfront development?

(a) whether Government has received a formal proposal from Government of West Bengal for expansion of 469 new Jana Aushadhi Kendra and if so, the current status of examination of such proposal by Bureau of Pharma PSUs of the country;
(b) the district-wise and block-wise number of PM Jan Aushadhi Kendras currently operational in West Bengal and the number and names of community development Blocks in the State that do not have even a single Jan Aushadhi Kendra, given that West Bengal has 341 community development blocks across 23 districts; and
(c) the timeline within which the proposed expansion of 469 Kendras will be sanctioned and operationalised?

(a) the physical and financial status of Teesta Barrage Project under PMKSY-AIBP — envisaged to irrigate 3.42 lakh hectares across Coochbehar, Jalpaiguri, Darjeeling and Uttar Dinajpur including Central funds sanctioned, released and utilised and irrigation potential created;
(b) whether Government has received the Detailed Project Report from West Bengal for a revised underground pipeline-based irrigation network to overcome land acquisition bottlenecks and its appraisal status at Central Water Commission; and
(c) the timeline for approval and sanction under PMKSY-AIBP given that large tracts of North Bengal farmland remain unirrigated despite land being acquired under the project decades ago?

(a) whether AAI has received formal land transfer from Government of West Bengal for the proposed civil enclave at Kalaikunda Air Force Station, for which 38.25 acres of land was sought, stalled over a decade due to previous State Government's refusal;
(b) status of proposed civil airport at Hasimara Air Force Station, Alipurduar, for which the new State Government has issued an NOC for 37 acres, including from adjoining tea estates and whether AAI has inspected the site and begun DPR preparation; and
(c) timeline, estimated project cost and routes envisaged for UDAN operationalisation at both airports?

(a) complete list of railway projects falling fully or partly in West Bengal that have been kept in abeyance at any point during the last five years, indicating the year of sanction, year placed in abeyance, original sanctioned cost and current estimated cost for each;
(b) specific reasons for each such project being kept in abeyance, including whether land acquisition failure, law and order issues or lack of cooperation by the State Government were the factors;
(c) cumulative cost escalation incurred on account of these projects remaining in abeyance, project-wise; and
(d) current status of the projects, whether any project has been revived, restarted or formally dropped?

(a) the sanctioned strength, working strength and vacancies of teaching and non-teaching staff at the Institute of Hotel Management, Catering Technology and Applied Nutrition, Kolkata, discipline-wise;
(b) whether the Institute is functioning with a significant dependence on contractual or guest faculty and the impact thereof on academic quality;
(c) the present status of academic, hostel and laboratory infrastructure and whether these facilities are adequate for the approved student intake;
(d) the details of funds sanctioned, released and utilized for infrastructure upgradation and faculty recruitment during the last five years; and
(e) whether Government has approved any time-bound plan to fill vacancies, modernize infrastructure and strengthen industry linkages at IHM Kolkata?

(a) whether Gurusaday Museum, Kolkata, under Development Commissioner (Handicrafts), remains recognised as an autonomous institution under Government of India and, if so, whether it has remained closed since June 2020 and the steps taken to restore its operations;
(b) whether its folk art collections have been or are proposed to be shifted to the Indian Museum, Kolkata, the present condition of such artefacts and whether disputes involving Bengal Bratachari Society over its land and collections are under Government's cognisance; and 
(c) whether Government proposes to resume active administrative and financial oversight to ensure preservation and public display of its cultural heritage?

(a) State-wise productivity per hectare of black tiger shrimp (Penaeus monodon) as per latest data, and whether West Bengal's ~0.38 MT/hectare/year is attributable to traditional extensive farming and inadequate Specific Pathogen Free (SPF) post-larvae availability;
(b) whether SPF broodstock infrastructure in eastern India is inadequate, forcing dependence on wild-caught broodstock carrying White Spot Syndrome Virus, and steps taken to address this;
(c) whether consignments from West Bengal have faced rejection in export markets including Japan and EU due to antibiotic residue; and
(d) funds allocated under PMMSY for West Bengal's Black Tiger Shrimp sector, including pond productivity and cluster-level export certification support?

(a) whether WBSEDCL and CESC consumers across West Bengal were denied benefits of Pradhan Mantri Surya Ghar: Muft Bijli Yojana (PMSGMBY) since its launch in February 2024 due to non-participation of the previous State Government and the estimated number of households deprived of central financial assistance during this period;
(b) the number of households in West Bengal that have registered, received feasibility approval, completed installation and been disbursed central financial assistance since the new State Government's rollout; and
(c) the steps taken to expedite WBSEDCL's portal integration and achieve the 2 lakh rooftop solar target, including the additional ?5,000 subsidy proposed for SC/ST beneficiaries?

(a) number of STPs sanctioned, under-construction and operational in West Bengal under Namami Gange, sewage treatment capacity created versus total sewage generated daily in Ganga-bank towns and whether West Bengal has the lowest STP progress among all States;
(b) total funds released and utilised by West Bengal under Namami Gange for STPs, whether land non-availability has been cited as the primary reason for delays despite 100 per cent Central funding and whether West Bengal has been directed to formulate a land purchase policy; and
(c) volume of untreated sewage entering the Ganga daily from West Bengal, whether this is undermining water quality gains in upstream States?

(a) whether the West Bengal Government has granted clearance for Road Over Bridges (ROBs) at 70 identified level crossing locations across the State; 
(b) if so, the specific locations along with their district-wise break-up and the number among these for which the Railway Board has so far issued formal sanction orders;
(c) the total funds allocated and released for ROB construction in West Bengal during financial years 2025-26 and 2026-27; and
(d) the timeline for taking up the remaining unsanctioned locations and the mechanism to ensure expeditious completion?

(a) whether 2,100-km Dankuni–Surat Dedicated Freight Corridor announced in the Union Budget 2026-27 is currently in the revised DPR phase, if so, the expected date by which the revised DPR with updated cost estimates and timelines will be finalised by DFCCIL;
(b) projected total project cost, the proposed funding mechanism and the timeline for commencement of construction and completion of the corridor; and
(c) status of pre-construction activities including land acquisition, identification of implementation sections, deployment of core teams and measures being taken to ensure that this strategically critical project connecting Dankuni in West Bengal with Surat in Gujarat does not face delays?

(a) whether it is a fact that only a survey of the Tamluk–Panskura 3rd and 4th line has been sanctioned to improve connectivity to Haldia Port, and that the project has not yet been sanctioned for execution;
(b) present status of the said survey, including whether DPR has been completed and reasons for delay in according sanction;
(c) whether congestion on the Panskura–Tamluk–Haldia section has been assessed in view of rising port cargo traffic;
(d) timeline proposed for sanction and commencement of the 3rd & 4th line works; and
(e) additional measures taken or proposed to strengthen rail evacuation capacity for Haldia Port?

(a) whether Government is aware that Gangasagar mela, the second largest religious congregation in India after Kumbh mela, drawing over one crore pilgrims annually at the confluence of river Ganga and Bay of Bengal, has not been granted national recognition;
(b) whether any formal proposal has been received from West Bengal Government to develop Gangasagar mela into an international spiritual and cultural festival jointly with the Centre, and if so, the current status thereof; and 
(c) the steps proposed by the Ministry, including infrastructure support, to develop Gangasagar as a premier pilgrimage destination on par with other nationally recognised fairs?

(a) whether Government has received formal proposal from West Bengal for an IIT and IIM in North Bengal, as announced in the State Budget 2026-27 presented on 22nd June, 2026;
(b) if so, the status of proposal, location-selection criteria within North Bengal and timeline for in-principle approval;
(c) if not, the procedural steps West Bengal must complete like land identification, DPR preparation and compliance with the Institutes of Technology Act, 1961 and IIM Act, 2017 before the Central approval; and
(d) Government's plan for equitable geographical distribution of IITs/IIMs, given North Bengal's strategic location?

(a) whether the Committee under Jal Shakti Ministry reviewed the gate operation mechanism of Farakka barrage and its impact on upstream and downstream erosion;
(b) whether any modification to the existing gate regulation schedule has been recommended;
(c) whether the proposed NHAI bridge downstream of Farakka is expected to increase pond levels by 10–12 cm during peak floods; and
(d) whether periodic five-year scientific modelling studies have been institutionalised as recommended in the report?

(a) the number of Software Technology Parks of India (STPI) units operational in West Bengal and the IT exports generated, in comparison to States like Karnataka, Telangana and Tamil Nadu;
(b) whether West Bengal's share of national IT exports has declined over the last decade despite having a large pool of technical graduates;
(c) whether the West Bengal Government's industrial and investment policies have been identified as a deterrent to IT sector investment; and
(d) the steps proposed by the Ministry to revive Kolkata as an IT/ITES hub and create employment for West Bengal youth?

(a) the present status of sports infrastructure, hostels and sports science facilities at SAI Kolkata and whether these are adequate for national-level training;
(b) the sanctioned strength, working strength and vacancies of coaches and support staff, discipline-wise;
(c) whether capacity at the Centre remains underutilised due to staffing, funding or infrastructure constraints;
(d) the funds sanctioned, released and utilised for upgradation and maintenance during the last three years; and
(e) the steps taken, with timelines, to fill vacancies, modernise facilities and strengthen athlete welfare and high-performance outcomes at SAI Kolkata?

(a) whether Government is aware that West Bengal is lagging behind in the implementation of Sukanya Samriddhi Scheme compared to other States in terms of population coverage;
(b) if so, the reasons for the lower number of accounts per capita in West Bengal compared to States like Uttar Pradesh, Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu;
(c) the steps taken by Government to improve awareness and adoption of this scheme in West Bengal, especially in rural areas; and
(d) whether banks and post offices have been instructed to create awareness among the public about the scheme, if so, the measures taken in this regard?

(a) whether West Bengal has installed only 5,45,586 of 2,07,17,969 sanctioned consumer smart meters, a mere 2.63 per cent, as on 31st December, 2025, making it the worst-performing State among all States;
(b) district-wise status of smart meter installation in West Bengal, including consumer meters, distribution transformer meters and feeder meters and the districts where installation progress is below even the State average of 2.63 per cent; and
(c) updated installation figures following Power Minister's visit to West Bengal in May, 2026 and the specific district-wise targets and monthly milestones committed to by the new State Government for smart meter deployment?

(a) whether Government is aware that West Bengal levies VAT plus additional tax on Aviation Turbine Fuel totalling an effective rate of about 24 per cent at Kolkata, the highest among major metro airports, against 1 per cent in Uttar Pradesh and Andhra Pradesh;
(b) whether this high ATF taxation has been identified as suppressing airline operations, route expansion and passenger growth at Kolkata airport;
(c) whether any representations have been made to the West Bengal Government to rationalise ATF VAT in line with the national average; and
(d) the estimated additional per-flight cost burden at Kolkata due to this differential taxation?
